**//Interview with D-Class Personnel #21749 on August 18th, 2024//**



[[div class="blockquote"]]



= **Interview Log 8.08.24**



**Interviewer:** Dr. X. Flip



**Interviewed:** D-Class Personnel #21749



**Foreword:** D-Class Personnel #21749 was specifically selected based on their lack of experience with personal loss to explore how the musical composition affects those not currently undergoing mourning.



----



(D-Class Personnel #21749 lays in their bed, staring up at the ceiling. Their foot taps the air rapidly, as their eyes dart around the room. As the door opens, they jerk up into a sitting position, facing Dr. X. Flip as he enters.)



**Dr. X. Flip:** Hello, #21749. How are we feeling today?



**D-Class #21749:** What- What did you take from me?



(Dr. Flip shoots back a look of confusion, as he takes a seat in front of them.)



**Dr. Flip:** What do you mean? Did someone remove something from your room?



**#21749:** No, you- you //took// something! I don't know what it is, but that song- It just...



(#21749 trails off.)



**Dr. Flip:** //ahem// Excuse me?



**#21749:** I'm sorry?



**Dr. Flip:** You were saying something about the song?



**#21749:** Oh, I...I'm sorry, it's hard to keep my head straight. I feel like there's a //million// thoughts running through my brain right now, and- I just **know** it has something to do with what you took from me!



**Dr. Flip:** How could I have possibly taken anything from you?



**#21749:** Oh, don't- don't do that! Don't play innocent! We all see what you do around here, and we understand more about it than you think we do. I know that you could do //something// to me that would- would make me feel like this- could take away something I needed! You //have// to tell me what it is!



**Dr. Flip:** Listen: I can help you, but you need to trust me: I did not "take" //anything// from you, alright? You are the same person you were before. Nothing is gone. //But//, I suppose the piece had quite the effect on you, considering your demeanor.



**#21749:** I can still hear it. It's like a- a **ringing**. That goes away, right?



**Dr. Flip:** Well-



(A short pause.)



**Dr. Flip:** Yes. It will. Now, please: guide me through your experience when the piece began.



**#21749:** (Deep exhale) Well, um...not long after it, uh, started, I felt, like, this instinct telling me to get the hell out of that room before it could keep going. It felt like if I didn't get out of there, I- I would've //changed//, or something.



(Dr. Flip leans forward in his chair, intrigued.)



**Dr. Flip:** So that's why you began pounding on the door.



**#21749:** Y-yeah, and- and I just wanted to get out of there //so badly//, I guess I- I got furious at you. For...forcing me to listen. And so I just kept banging and banging, and hoped that I could drown out the noise, but it- it pierced through **everything**.



But, I still, I- I felt that I needed to- to go. And I thought that- maybe I could do something //else// for you, something...that would get me out there. //Anything.// I seriously thought that- that I would give anything to get the fuck out of there. But nothing did. And, when I realized that there was nothing I could do, I- ...I just gave up. I felt like pounding on that door //one more time// would've taken m-more energy than I even had anymore. This song, //you//, it felt like, had taken everything away from me. Everything that was inside of me, just...gone. And that was just //completely// terrifying and, just **exhausting**.



(Dr. Flip, glued to #21749's expression, stares in a mix of awe and trepidation.)



**Dr. Flip:** Wow, I- I had no idea.



**#21749:** But then...I don't know, it was weird. This, like, feeling of calm just rushed through me, and, I just...listened. Not before long, it was all over, and you called me in here.



(A short pause.)



**#21749:** So, listen, I- I understand. I do. I realize why I went through all of this, your role in all of it, what this maybe even could be for. And, you know what? I don't even- I don't even really blame you. I just //really// need to know...what you took, or it is going to drive me //insane//. Please. Please tell me.



**Dr. Flip:** I- I'm sorry. I'm so sorry, but you haven't lost anything. Seriously. I don't even- I don't even **know** you. This piece, it's- it's meant for people who //have//, but-



(#21749 simply stares back at him, tearing up. Dr. Flip abruptly stands up.)



**Dr. Flip:** You have to believe me, I'm so- I never intended for you to feel this way.



(Dr. Flip turns and begins walking toward the door.)



**#21749:** Isn't there anything? Anything you could do to- to help me?



**Dr. Flip:** We're uh, still actively exploring other methods to lessen the effects.



**#21749:** But, wait! -



(Dr. Flip exits the chamber, the door slamming shut and locking behind him.)



----



**Afterword:** SCP-9250 appears to have similar emotional effects during the piece on those who haven't experienced personal loss, but leads to extreme confusion once all movements have concluded. The subject seems to have accepted losing someone that did not exist, and cannot reconcile their emotions with objective reality.



